What is Load Balancer ?
=========================
A Load Balancer is a service or software that distributes incoming network traffic across multiple servers so that no single server becomes overloaded.

Why do we need a Load Balancer?
===================================
Imagine you have a website hosted on one server:

		Users request to website ---> Server-1
		
		
If 10,000 users visit at the same time, Server-1 may become slow or crash.


              +----------+
Users ------> | Load     |
              | Balancer |
              +----------+
                /   |   \
               /    |    \
         Server1 Server2 Server3
		 
		 
The load balancer receives all requests and forwards them to available servers.		 
	

Common Load Balancing Algorithms
=================================
1. Round Robin
---------------
Requests are distributed one by one.

Req1 → Server1
Req2 → Server2
Req3 → Server3
Req4 → Server1

Most commonly used.



2. Least Connections
--------------------

Traffic goes to the server with the fewest active connections.

Server1 = 100 connections
Server2 = 20 connections

Next request → Server2


3. Weighted Round Robin
------------------------
More powerful servers receive more requests.

Example:

Server1 Weight = 5
Server2 Weight = 2
Server3 Weight = 1

Server1 gets the majority of traffic.


Types of Load Balancers
=========================
Layer 4 Load Balancer (Transport Layer)

Works with:

	TCP
	UDP

Looks only at IP addresses and ports.

Example:

Client ---> Load Balancer ---> Web Servers

Fast but less intelligent.



Layer 7 Load Balancer (Application Layer)
=========================================
Works with:

HTTP
HTTPS

Can make decisions based on:

URL
Hostname
Headers
Cookies

Example:

/api/*      → API Servers
/images/*   → Image Servers



